## Modifying Bean Body Content

Use `beans update` to modify body content along with metadata changes:

**Replace text (exact match, must occur exactly once):**
```bash
beans update <id> --body-replace-old "- [ ] Task 1" --body-replace-new "- [x] Task 1"
```
- Errors if text not found or found multiple times
- Use empty string to delete the matched text

**Append content:**
```bash
beans update <id> --body-append "## Notes\n\nAdded content"
echo "Multi-line content" | beans update <id> --body-append -
```
- Adds text to end of body with blank line separator
- Use `-` to read from stdin

**Combined with metadata changes:**
```bash
beans update <id> \
  --body-replace-old "- [ ] Deploy to prod" --body-replace-new "- [x] Deploy to prod" \
  --status completed
```

**Multiple replacements (via GraphQL):**
```bash
beans query 'mutation {
  updateBean(id: "<id>", input: {
    status: "completed"
    bodyMod: {
      replace: [
        { old: "- [ ] Task 1", new: "- [x] Task 1" }
        { old: "- [ ] Task 2", new: "- [x] Task 2" }
      ]
      append: "## Summary\n\nAll tasks completed!"
    }
  }) { id body etag }
}'
```
- Replacements execute sequentially (each operates on result of previous)
- Append applied after all replacements
- All operations atomic with single etag validation
- Transactional: any failure = no changes saved

## Custom Front Matter

Beans preserve front matter keys outside the known schema (title, status, type, priority, tags, parent, blocking, blocked_by, order) instead of dropping them. Use this for project-specific metadata that doesn't need its own flag -- a release cut, a classification, anything the built-in fields don't cover.

```bash
# Set one or more extra keys (repeatable, works on create and update)
beans create --json "Title" -t task --set release=0-5-0 --set klasse=bugfix
beans update --json <id> --set release=0-6-0

# Remove an extra key
beans update --json <id> --unset release

# Filter by an extra key (AND logic across repeats)
beans list --json --where release=0-5-0
```

- A key that names a known field (e.g. `--set status=done`) is rejected -- use the native flag (`--status`) instead.
- Extra keys appear in `list`/`show --json` under an `extra` object, and are queryable over GraphQL.
- This is metadata, not a new hierarchy level: use it for cross-cutting groupings (like a release) that don't map onto the milestone → epic → task parent chain.

{{if .RequiredFields}}
## Required Fields on Status Transitions

This project's `.beans.yml` requires the following front matter field(s) before a bean can enter a given status. `beans complete`/`beans update`/`beans create` reject an unqualified transition into a listed status with a `POLICY_VIOLATION` error naming what's missing.
{{range .RequiredFields}}
- **{{.Status}}**: {{range $i, $f := .Fields}}{{if $i}}, {{end}}`{{$f}}`{{end}}
{{- end}}
{{if .CommitFieldGated}}
`{{.CommitField}}` is resolved automatically -- pass a git ref and beans records the full 40-character SHA in one write:

```bash
{{if .CompletionCommitGated}}beans complete <id> --commit <ref> --summary "what was done"
{{end}}beans update <id> --set {{.CommitField}}=<ref> ...   # combine with whichever status change requires it
beans create --json "Title" -t task --set {{.CommitField}}=<ref> ...
```

`<ref>` accepts anything `git rev-parse --verify <ref>^{commit}` resolves (`HEAD`, a branch, tag, short SHA); an unresolvable ref fails before any write, and no partial state is saved.
{{end}}
Any other required field needs a plain `--set <field>=<value>` in the same write as the status change.

Surface every policy violation across the workspace: `beans check --strict` (warnings show by default; `--strict` counts them as issues and exits non-zero). A bean already sitting in a gated status without the field (e.g. pre-policy data) can still receive maintenance writes -- rename, body edits -- as long as its status doesn't change; the policy only gates the transition itself.
{{end}}

## Manual Ordering

Beans carry an optional `order` field (a fractional-index string) for manual sequencing among siblings under the same parent.

```bash
# Place a bean relative to its siblings
beans order <id> --after <sibling-id>   # directly after a sibling that already has an order
beans order <id> --before <sibling-id>  # directly before a sibling that already has an order
beans order <id> --first                # before every sibling
beans order <id> --last                 # after every sibling

# List in manual order (siblings without an order sort last)
beans list --json --sort order

# Set an explicit order value at creation time
beans create --json "Title" -t task --order V
```

- Ordering is scoped per parent: it only compares siblings that share the same `parent`.
- Placing a bean relative to (`--after`/`--before`) a sibling that has no order value yet fails -- place that sibling first (e.g. with `--last`).
- A move writes exactly one file: the bean being placed, never its neighbours.

## Failure Output

Every command exits 1 on failure and describes it in exactly one place — which
place depends on whether you passed `--json`.

Without `--json`, two lines go to stderr and stdout stays empty:

```
Error: parent bean not found: beans-nope
Run '<command> --help' for usage.
```

With `--json`, one document goes to stdout and stderr stays empty:

```json
{"success": false, "error": "parent bean not found: beans-nope", "code": "VALIDATION_ERROR"}
```

Codes: `NOT_FOUND`, `NO_BEANS_DIR`, `INVALID_STATUS`, `FILE_ERROR`,
`VALIDATION_ERROR`, `CONFLICT`, `POLICY_VIOLATION`.

So: under `--json`, parse stdout and branch on `success`; an empty stderr is
normal and not a sign that something went missing. A success payload never
carries an `error` key, so the two are unambiguous. No command prints a usage
block on failure — if you want the flags, ask for them with `--help`.

A batch verb (`complete`, `scrap`, `start`, `tag`, `delete`) that fails partway
through returns `{"success": false, "beans": [...], "count": n, ...}` — the
beans listed there were already written before the failure struck.

## Concurrency Control

Use etags with `--if-match`:
```bash
ETAG=$(beans show <id> --etag-only)
beans update <id> --if-match "$ETAG" ...
```

On conflict, returns an error with the current etag.

## GraphQL Queries

The `beans query` command allows advanced querying using GraphQL.

- Fetch exactly the fields you need, across a potentially large set of beans
- Directly read all fields (including `body`) and relationships
- Traverse relationships in a single query
- Execute mutations to create and update beans
- `beans query --help` for syntax and usage details
- `beans query --schema` to view the full GraphQL schema

```bash
# Get all actionable beans with their details
beans query --json '{ beans(filter: { excludeStatus: ["completed", "scrapped"], isBlocked: false }) { id title status type body } }'

# Get a single bean with its relationships
beans query --json '{ bean(id: "bean-abc") { title body parent { title } children { id title status } } }'

# Find high-priority bugs
beans query --json '{ beans(filter: { type: ["bug"], priority: ["critical", "high"] }) { id title } }'

# Search with text
beans query --json '{ beans(filter: { search: "authentication" }) { id title body } }'
```
